{title: It Is Well with My Soul}
{lyricist: Horatio G. Spafford}
{composer: Philip P. Bliss}
{year: 1873}
{meta: tune-year 1876}
{key: C}
{time: 4/4}
{meta: tune VILLE DU HAVRE}
{meta: variation The hymn, sung to VILLE DU HAVRE}
{meta: license public-domain}
{meta: tags peace, trust, comfort}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
When [C]peace like a [F]river at[C]tendeth my way,
When [C]sorrows like [Am]sea billows [D7]roll; [G]
What[G7]ever my [C]lot, Thou hast [F]taught me to [C]say,
It is [F]well, it is [G7]well with my [C]soul.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus: Refrain}
It is [G7]well (it is [C]well)
With my [G7]soul (with my [C]soul),
It is [F]well, it is [C]well [G7]with my [C]soul.
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
Though [C]Satan should [F]buffet, though [C]trials should come,
Let [C]this blest as[Am]surance con[D7]trol, [G]
That [G7]Christ hath re[C]garded my [F]helpless es[C]tate,
And hath [F]shed His own [G7]blood for my [C]soul.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
My [C]sin, oh, the [F]bliss of this [C]glorious thought!
My [C]sin, not in [Am]part but the [D7]whole, [G]
Is [G7]nailed to the [C]cross, and I [F]bear it no [C]more,
Praise the [F]Lord, praise the [G7]Lord, O my [C]soul!
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
And [C]Lord, haste the [F]day when my [C]faith shall be sight,
The [C]clouds be rolled [Am]back as a [D7]scroll; [G]
The [G7]trump shall re[C]sound, and the [F]Lord shall de[C]scend,
Even [F]so, it is [G7]well with my [C]soul.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}
